✅ Q1: Identify the violated design principle + Give solution
Problem

Interface Machine forces every class to implement print(), scan(), fax() even if the machine cannot support all functions (like OldPrinter).

🔍 Violated Design Principle

👉 Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
(“Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.”)

OldPrinter is forced to implement scan() and fax() even though it doesn’t support them → violation.

✅ Correct Solution (Refactored Code Using ISP)
Step 1 — Break big interface into small interfaces
interface Printer {
    void print();
}

interface Scanner {
    void scan();
}

interface Fax {
    void fax();
}

Step 2 — Only implement what each machine supports
class OldPrinter implements Printer {
    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing document...");
    }
}

class MultiFunctionPrinter implements Printer, Scanner, Fax {
    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing...");
    }
    @Override
    public void scan() {
        System.out.println("Scanning...");
    }
    @Override
    public void fax() {
        System.out.println("Faxing...");
    }
}


✔ Problem solved — no class is forced to implement unnecessary methods.

✅ Q2: Java Program for Average of 5 Subjects + Grade
Grading Criteria (simple example)

A → avg ≥ 85

B → avg ≥ 70

C → avg ≥ 60

D → avg ≥ 50

F → below 50

PROGRAM
import java.util.Scanner;

public class StudentGrade {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int s1, s2, s3, s4, s5;

        System.out.print("Enter marks of 5 subjects: ");
        s1 = sc.nextInt();
        s2 = sc.nextInt();
        s3 = sc.nextInt();
        s4 = sc.nextInt();
        s5 = sc.nextInt();

        double avg = (s1 + s2 + s3 + s4 + s5) / 5.0;

        System.out.println("Average = " + avg);

        if (avg >= 85)
            System.out.println("Grade: A");
        else if (avg >= 70)
            System.out.println("Grade: B");
        else if (avg >= 60)
            System.out.println("Grade: C");
        else if (avg >= 50)
            System.out.println("Grade: D");
        else
            System.out.println("Grade: F");
    }
}

✅ Q3: Identify the code smell + Refactor
Given Problem

Penguin extends Bird, but Bird has fly().
Penguins cannot fly → overriding method throws exception → LSP violation.

🔍 Code Smell

👉 Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) Violation
👉 Also Wrong Inheritance / Misplaced Method
👉 Refused Bequest

🔧 Correct Refactored Solution
Step 1 — Birds should not all have fly()
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

Step 2 — Bird base class without fly()
class Bird {
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating...");
    }
}

Step 3 — Flying birds implement Flyable
class Sparrow extends Bird implements Flyable {
    @Override
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Sparrow flying...");
    }
}

Step 4 — Penguin does NOT implement Flyable
class Penguin extends Bird {
    public void swim() {
        System.out.println("Penguin swimming...");
    }
}
